+## Program Overview
+
+This four-week after-school program serves a small mixed-readiness group of Grade 6 readers using engaging, science-themed informational texts. The group meets for two 45-minute sessions each week, on Monday and Wednesday. Instruction combines teacher modeling, supported partner work, and increasingly independent reading. Across the eight sessions, students practice identifying main ideas and supporting details, using context clues to understand academic vocabulary, and citing textual evidence. Brief read-alouds, chunked text, optional partner rehearsal, large-print or digital copies, and sentence frames provide access without changing the learning targets.
+
+Each session follows a practical rhythm: a five-minute retrieval warm-up, a ten-minute mini-lesson, twenty minutes of guided or collaborative reading, five minutes of independent application, and a five-minute exit ticket. Evidence from each meeting informs grouping and the amount of modeling supplied at the next session.
+
+## Learning Goals
+
+- By the end of Week 4, students will identify the main idea and select at least three accurate supporting details on 4 of 5 informational-text items.
+- By the end of Week 4, students will use context clues to determine the meaning of academic vocabulary and explain the clue used with at least 80% accuracy.
+- By the end of Week 4, students will answer a science-text question by citing relevant textual evidence and explaining its connection on at least 3 of 4 rubric criteria.
+- Students will increase independence by moving from modeled annotation to partner practice and then to an unfamiliar-text transfer task while using the same reading routines.
+
+## Four-Week Schedule
+
+**Week 1 — Main ideas in ecosystems**
+
+- **Monday (45 minutes)**
+  - Objective: Identify the topic and distinguish a main idea from an interesting detail in a short article about pond ecosystems.
+  - Activity: Complete the initial diagnostic, watch a teacher think-aloud that uses headings and repeated ideas, and sort prepared statements into “main idea” and “detail” categories with a partner.
+  - Evidence: Submit a one-sentence main idea and underline two supporting details; the teacher records diagnostic accuracy and the level of prompting used.
+- **Wednesday (45 minutes)**
+  - Objective: Select supporting details that directly explain or prove a stated main idea in an article about desert habitats.
+  - Activity: Reread two chunked paragraphs, annotate repeated concepts, and use a main-idea/details organizer first with a partner and then independently.
+  - Evidence: Complete the organizer with one defensible main idea and three relevant supporting details, followed by the session exit ticket.
+
+**Week 2 — Vocabulary in weather science**
+
+- **Monday (45 minutes)**
+  - Objective: Use definition and example context clues to infer academic vocabulary in a short text about the water cycle.
+  - Activity: Study a modeled clue hunt for “condensation,” then highlight clue words and complete vocabulary organizers for three new terms in pairs.
+  - Evidence: Record a meaning and the exact clue used for each term; demonstrate at least three correct inferences on the session exit ticket.
+- **Wednesday (45 minutes)**
+  - Objective: Use contrast, synonym, and cause-and-effect context clues to explain weather vocabulary without relying first on a glossary.
+  - Activity: Read a leveled article about severe weather, label the type of context clue around four bold terms, and compare explanations during partner rehearsal.
+  - Evidence: Independently explain two word meanings and identify the supporting clue in writing, followed by the session exit ticket.
+
+**Week 3 — Citing evidence in space science**
+
+- **Monday (45 minutes)**
+  - Objective: Choose relevant textual evidence to answer a question about how solar energy affects a planet’s surface.
+  - Activity: Observe a model of matching a claim to a quotation, use an evidence tracker to compare strong and weak choices, and select evidence from a short space-science article.
+  - Evidence: Produce one answer containing a claim and an accurately copied or paraphrased detail, then explain orally why that detail is relevant.
+- **Wednesday (45 minutes)**
+  - Objective: Cite textual evidence and explain how it supports an inference in an article about conditions on Mars.
+  - Activity: Annotate the question, gather two possible details with a partner, and use a sentence frame to write an evidence-based response before revising independently.
+  - Evidence: Submit a claim-evidence-reasoning paragraph scored for relevance and connection, followed by the session exit ticket.
+
+**Week 4 — Integrating skills through engineering**
+
+- **Monday (45 minutes)**
+  - Objective: Integrate main idea, supporting detail, context clue, vocabulary, and textual evidence strategies in an article about wind-turbine engineering.
+  - Activity: Rotate through three stations: complete a main-idea organizer, solve two vocabulary-in-context items, and build an evidence tracker for a design question.
+  - Evidence: Finish all three station products and use a checklist to identify one skill that needs a final review before the transfer task.
+- **Wednesday (45 minutes)**
+  - Objective: Independently transfer all three reading skills to an unfamiliar informational text about energy-saving building design.
+  - Activity: Read and annotate the new article, write its main idea with three supporting details, infer two academic words from context, and compose an evidence-based response.
+  - Evidence: Submit the completed end-of-program transfer task for the 4-point rubric and complete a final reflection naming one effective reading strategy.
+
+## Assessment
+
+- **Initial diagnostic:** During Week 1 Monday, students read a brief science passage and complete one main-idea item, three supporting-detail selections, two context-clue items, and one evidence-based response. The teacher records accuracy and prompting needs to form flexible pairs and choose supports.
+- **Ongoing observation:** The teacher uses a simple roster checklist during guided work to note correct strategy use, misconceptions, independence, and useful verbal explanations. Work samples from organizers and evidence trackers are retained in individual folders.
+- **Exit ticket after every session:** Each five-minute ticket contains one item tied to that day’s objective and one confidence check. The teacher reviews all tickets before the next meeting, reteaches patterns of error in the next warm-up, and offers a scaffolded or extension version as needed.
+- **End-of-program transfer task:** On Week 4 Wednesday, each student independently reads an unfamiliar science informational text, states the main idea with supporting details, infers vocabulary using identified context clues, and answers a question with cited textual evidence and reasoning.
+- **4-point rubric:** Score the transfer task as follows.
+  - **4 — Secure:** Accurate main idea with three relevant details, accurate context-clue reasoning, and well-chosen evidence with a clear explanation.
+  - **3 — Developing securely:** Accurate work in all three skills with one minor omission or imprecise explanation.
+  - **2 — Emerging:** Partial success in at least two skills, but details, clue reasoning, or the evidence connection needs support.
+  - **1 — Beginning:** Limited success across the skills; the response requires renewed modeling and guided practice.
+- Compare the diagnostic and transfer-task evidence by skill, then note one demonstrated strength and one next instructional step for each student.
+
+## Materials
+
+- Eight short science articles covering ecosystems, habitats, the water cycle, severe weather, planets, Mars, wind turbines, and energy-saving buildings, supplied in print and in large-print or digital versions.
+- Main-idea and supporting-detail organizers, vocabulary organizers, claim-evidence-reasoning frames, and reusable evidence trackers.
+- Annotation supplies such as highlighters, colored pencils, sticky notes, pencils, and optional transparent reading guides.
+- Prepared statement-sort cards, context-clue labels, student folders, session exit tickets, the initial diagnostic, and copies of the 4-point rubric.
+- A board or display for teacher modeling and optional text-to-speech access for digital articles while students continue to work toward the same comprehension goals.
